@page "/exgrid/clipboard"
@using ExGrid.Chrome
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid

<DocsPage Product="ExGrid" Title="Clipboard"
          Lead="Copy and paste behave as a spreadsheet user expects, with Excel on the other end. Where the result would be quietly wrong, the grid refuses instead: a copy is never truncated, and a paste never writes outside the selection.">

    <DocsSection Title="Copy">
        <p>
            Select cells and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Insert</kbd>); the Context Menu (right-click, or
            <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F10</kbd>) also offers <em>Copy with headers</em>, and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>
            (<kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> on a Mac) runs it from the keyboard, as SQL Server Management Studio's
            results grid does. Nothing needs wiring.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Two formats go on the clipboard.</strong> The text each cell shows, its <code>format</code>
                applied, as plain text; and the raw values as an HTML table, so Excel receives every digit of an
                amount the grid displays rounded. A cell showing <code>####</code> copies its value, never the hashes.
                <em>Copy with headers</em> puts each column's header in both. The key is the grid's only while it
                offers the item: with <code>hideCopyWithHeaders</code> (<code>HideCopyWithHeaders</code>) it stays the
                browser's, which opens its element inspector by it, and you may declare it yourself. In Blazor the key
                is <code>ExGrid.Script.ExGrid</code>'s; the C# core's grid offers Copy with headers from its menu only.
                When you answer a copy yourself,
                <code>ClipboardData.Assemble</code> makes the two formats from each cell's shown and raw text, and a
                header that shows a number rounded can carry the number in full for the HTML through
                <code>headerRawAt</code>, as ExPivot's number column Items do.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Rows off screen are copied too.</strong> A selection larger than the rows the grid has painted
                asks the source for the rest; a source of your own answers them through <code>GetRowsAsync</code>. A
                copy whose rows cannot be read is refused, not cut short.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Several ranges copy as one block</strong> when they line up — the same rows, or the same
                columns — as in Excel.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Your application can answer a copy itself.</strong> Set <code>CopyAnswer</code> and, once the
                grid's own rules have approved a copy, it is handed the ranges being copied
                (<code>GridCopyRequest</code>, with the Row Sequence Version their positions are read under) and the
                grid writes exactly what it returns: <code>GridCopyAnswer.Write(text, html)</code>, or
                <code>GridCopyAnswer.Refuse(sentence)</code>, which leaves the clipboard as it was, announces your
                sentence and raises <code>OnRefused</code> with a <code>GridRefusal.Copy</code> of <code>RefusedByConsumer</code>. It is asked
                synchronously, in the same process, so it answers from data you hold — ExSheet answers its copies
                this way, from the values its formulas give. <code>CopyAnswerAsync</code> is the same answer for data
                you must ask for first, such as a report a server computes, which is how ExPivot copies: the copy
                then always takes the clipboard's asynchronous route, even when its cells are on screen. Note the
                version of your data before you await, and answer from that version or refuse — the grid never fills
                a gap with what it happens to hold now. When both are set, <code>CopyAnswer</code> is the one asked.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>What you copied stays outlined with dashes</strong> while the clipboard still holds it, as
                Excel's moving border does, but standing still. It goes when anything else is copied — in another
                application, another grid or a text field — when you press <kbd>Escape</kbd> or start an edit, and
                when a copied cell comes to show another value. A paste leaves it. A browser that cannot tell the
                page the clipboard changed (Chrome and Edge before 143) shows no outline at all.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A numeric header keeps its precision">
        <DocsExample Title="Rounded header, complete number" Builtin="typeof(NumericHeaders)" Mud="typeof(NumericHeadersMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/NumericHeaderCopy.cs"])">
            Select the cells, open the Context Menu and choose <em>Copy with headers</em>. A text editor receives
            the header <code>1.23457E+11</code> as shown; Excel receives <code>123456789012.345</code> from the HTML
            table. This Consumer answers through <code>CopyAnswer</code>, using <code>headerRawAt</code> for the
            numeric header and returning <code>null</code> for Sample, whose header is the same in both formats. In
            React the same answer is <code>copyAnswer</code>, built with <code>assembleCopy</code> from
            <code>@@ex-grid/core</code>, and <code>undefined</code> stands for <code>null</code>.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Why a copy refuses rather than truncates">
        <p>
            The everyday use of a blotter is to select rows, paste them into Excel and total them there. If a copy
            quietly kept only the first thousand rows, the total would be too small and nothing on screen would say
            so. So past <code>CopyCellCap</code> — a million cells by default — the grid copies <strong>nothing</strong>,
            leaves the clipboard as it was, says so (below), and raises <code>OnRefused</code> with a
            <code>GridRefusal.Copy</code> naming the reason:
            <code>TooLarge</code>, <code>MisalignedShape</code>, <code>EmptySelection</code>, and a few more. A
            selection of exactly the cap still copies.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Paste">
        <p>
            Paste — <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd>, or <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Insert</kbd> — into editable columns raises one <code>OnPaste</code> for the whole block: the target ranges and a
            value for every target cell. As with an edit, the grid writes nothing — your application applies it by
            replacing rows. Call <code>Refuse()</code> on the intent to decline it, and the selection stays put.
            Only two of Excel's four shape rules are kept:
        </p>
        <MudSimpleTable Dense="true" Outlined="true" Class="docs-api-table">
            <thead><tr><th style="width: 40%">Copied → selected</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>One cell → a range</td><td>The value fills every selected cell, across disjoint ranges too.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>2 rows → 6 rows</td><td>The block repeats to fill the selection: a target of M×N accepts a block of m×n when m divides M and n divides N.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>A block → one cell</td><td><strong>Refused</strong> (<code>SingleCellTarget</code>). Excel would spill past the selection, into rows that may not even be on screen. Select a range of the block's shape.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>3 rows → 5 rows</td><td><strong>Refused</strong> (<code>ShapeMismatch</code>), as in Excel.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </MudSimpleTable>
        <p>
            A selection that covers a column which is not editable is refused first, as
            <code>TargetNotEditable</code>, since no reselection of the same shape would help. A single value pasted
            as plain text — not as a table — over a range lands in its top-left cell only. A table copied from a web
            page is read cell by cell in the order its cells stand, row headings included. Numbers copied from Excel
            arrive at full precision, and <code>Origins</code> tells you whether a field is invariant-culture text
            from Excel's own data or the grid's, or text as it was shown. Ctrl+D and Ctrl+R hand you the raw values
            of the range they read, so their fields are invariant throughout; Ctrl+Enter's are text as typed. In the family's own HTML a cell may carry
            <code>ClipboardData.MarkAttribute</code>; <code>MarkFor</code> hands its value to the Consumer that
            wrote it, and another program ignores it. ExSheet marks a refused cell so that a paste into a Sheet is
            refused.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Copy out, paste in" Builtin="typeof(ClipboardGrid)" Mud="typeof(ClipboardGridMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/Position.cs"])">
            This grid's cap is set to 2,000 cells so the refusal is easy to see: press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd>,
            then <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>. Then select a few rows and copy again. Quantity and Price take a paste;
            the other columns refuse it. The grid announces each refusal itself; this Example replaces the words for
            two of them through the label function, and shows the reason beneath the grid.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A paste under way and Escape">
        <p>
            A large paste on a Blazor Server connection takes a moment to cross: the clipboard's data is read
            over the circuit before anything is decided. It crosses gzip-compressed, so a spreadsheet's text
            takes about a tenth of the time it would as it is; a ~10 MB paste over a 150 ms round trip lands in
            a few seconds. From the paste's start until it lands or is refused the
            grid says it is <strong>busy</strong>. Its root carries <code>aria-busy="true"</code>, a bar runs
            across its top edge with a progress pointer (the <code>--ex-pasting-*</code> tokens on Appearance),
            and the live region says once: “@BuiltInCommandLabels.For(GridLabelIds.PasteUnderWay)” The keys typed
            meanwhile wait their turn behind the paste, as every input does.
        </p>
        <p>
            <kbd>Escape</kbd>, pressed then, <strong>cancels the paste</strong> and does nothing else. If the grid
            has not yet raised the paste's Edit Intent, nothing is written and the paste is refused as
            <code>PasteRefusalReason.Cancelled</code>, through <code>OnRefused</code> like any refusal. If
            the intent was already raised, your handler has the paste, it stands, and the grid says so:
            “@BuiltInCommandLabels.For(GridLabelIds.PasteAlreadyLanded)” Which of the two you get depends on
            when the key arrives, the one outcome in the grid that does; either is said aloud. On WebAssembly a
            paste is read at once, so there is seldom anything under way to cancel.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="What a refusal sounds like">
        <p>
            Every refused copy and every refused paste, fill or <kbd>Delete</kbd> is <strong>announced</strong> as it
            happens, once, in the grid's own live region — politely, so a screen reader says it after what it is
            saying — in English sentences that say what to do next where there is something to do. Nothing needs
            wiring, and nothing is drawn: the region is visually hidden. A few of them:
        </p>
        <MudSimpleTable Dense="true" Outlined="true" Class="docs-api-table">
            <thead><tr><th style="width: 30%">Reason</th><th>What is announced</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>SingleCellTarget</code></td><td>@BuiltInCommandLabels.For(RefusalLabelIds.PasteSingleCellTarget)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ShapeMismatch</code></td><td>@BuiltInCommandLabels.For(RefusalLabelIds.PasteShapeMismatch)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ShapeMismatch</code>, with <code>PasteMaySpill</code></td><td>@BuiltInCommandLabels.For(RefusalLabelIds.PasteShapeMismatchMaySpill) — where a paste may spill, one cell takes the block from its top-left, so the words offer it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>TargetNotEditable</code></td><td>@BuiltInCommandLabels.For(RefusalLabelIds.PasteTargetNotEditable).Replace(RefusalLabelIds.ColumnPlaceholder, "Desk") — naming the column that refused, by its header, or by its name where the header is blank, as a Mark Column's is.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>TooLarge</code> (copy)</td><td>@BuiltInCommandLabels.For(RefusalLabelIds.CopyTooLarge)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ClipboardUnavailable</code></td><td>@BuiltInCommandLabels.For(RefusalLabelIds.CopyClipboardUnavailable)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Cancelled</code></td><td>@BuiltInCommandLabels.For(RefusalLabelIds.PasteCancelled) — <kbd>Escape</kbd> was pressed while the paste was still being read, so nothing is written.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Unreadable</code></td><td>@BuiltInCommandLabels.For(RefusalLabelIds.PasteUnreadable) — the clipboard's data stopped arriving before it was read whole, as a large paste can on a slow Blazor Server connection. Nothing is pasted from part of it, and the grid goes on answering.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </MudSimpleTable>
        <p>
            The words are the built-in Chrome's, keyed by the reason, and replaced the way its menu labels are:
            through <code>CommandLabel</code>, which is asked with each reason's id in <code>RefusalLabelIds</code>
            — a shape mismatch by <code>PasteShapeMismatchMaySpill</code> on a grid with <code>PasteMaySpill</code>.
            Answer your own sentence to replace one, <code>null</code> to keep the English, and the empty string to
            silence one — you then own saying it. Where the words hold <code>{column}</code>
            (<code>RefusalLabelIds.ColumnPlaceholder</code>), the grid writes the refusing column's header. Under
            ExGrid.MudBlazor the Chrome's <code>Label</code> answers the same ids (MudBlazor has no localised text
            for these), and a Chrome of your own answers them through <code>IGridChrome.Words</code>. A copy past
            the cap points at export, never at a smaller selection, for the reason above: if your page has no
            export, replace those words with what it does offer.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>OnRefused</code> still receives the reason, whatever was said, and binding it silences nothing. If you also show a refusal on the page, put it in an element that is
            not a live region, or it is announced twice. A sentence your <code>CopyAnswer</code> refuses with is
            announced in place of the words for <code>RefusedByConsumer</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            In React the grid's <code>commandLabel</code> is asked with the same ids, which <code>@@ex-grid/core</code>
            exports as <code>copyRefusalLabelIds</code>, <code>pasteRefusalLabelIds</code> and
            <code>shapeMismatchMaySpillLabelId</code>, with <code>refusalColumnPlaceholder</code> for the column;
            <code>undefined</code> keeps the English, the empty string silences one, and <code>onRefused</code>
            still receives the reason. <code>japaneseGridWords</code> answers every one of them.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="API">
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(ExGrid.Chrome.RefusalLabelIds)" />
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(ExGrid.Clipboard.GridPasteIntent)" />
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(ExGrid.Clipboard.PastePlan)" />
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
